WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.600
Chapter two. Where the edge actually came

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:05.300
from. So the honest version of this is quite

3
00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:07.340
boring. There was no course,

4
00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:10.280
no certification, nothing you could put on

5
00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.320
a CV. It was YouTube.

6
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.460
Specifically, it was watching people build

7
00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:17.880
real things and then copying what they did.

8
00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:20.240
And here is the part that matters,

9
00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:22.640
because it changes which videos are worth

10
00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:25.380
your time. The useful channels are the ones

11
00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:27.320
where something goes wrong on camera.

12
00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:29.000
Where the thing breaks,

13
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.080
and you watch somebody work out why.

14
00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:33.480
Anyone can film a tool working.

15
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.260
That teaches you almost nothing,

16
00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.700
because your version will not work the first

17
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:40.120
time either. Watching somebody diagnose a

18
00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.320
failure is the part you can actually use on

19
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.060
Tuesday morning. Three rules before you press

20
00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.320
play on any of this.

21
00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.480
First. Have the thing open while you watch.

22
00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:52.140
Passive viewing teaches you vocabulary and

23
00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.480
nothing else. Pause every few minutes,

24
00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:56.600
run the same step on something of your own,

25
00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.400
and watch it behave differently to how it

26
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.520
did on screen. Second.

27
00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:03.760
Pick one tool and stay with it for a month.

28
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.960
The channels will push something new every

29
00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.300
single week, because that is their business

30
00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:11.160
model. Depth in one beats a tour of nine,

31
00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.320
every time. Third. Check the upload date.

32
00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.380
Anything past about six months has aged out

33
00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:18.220
of being instruction.

34
00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:20.300
The ideas usually survive.

35
00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.980
The buttons have almost certainly moved.

36
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:26.060
The list in this chapter is sixteen videos

37
00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.520
and four channels, sorted by how long each

38
00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.560
one takes. Start at the top.

39
00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.100
About thirty minutes of it will put you ahead

40
00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:34.240
of most people in the room.

